pages="431--448",
abstract="Preparing teachers for emotionally charged classroom disruptions is still largely based on in-person roleplays, which are difficult to standardize and resource-intensive to facilitate. To address this gap, we developed an immersive VR training application that enables safe, repeatable practice with disruptive classroom scenarios. The system supports real-time spoken interaction with AI-driven student avatars and adapts dynamically to teacher interventions. At its core, a single generative model coordinates the full class as a coherent social simulation, while auxiliary modules manage disruption escalation and generate formative feedback on teacher responses. A pilot study with 15 participants showed high immersion and emotional engagement, supporting the feasibility of VR for emotion-regulation training.",
isbn="978-3-032-30542-8"
}

